rests:
// A shipment run by GitHub Actions: a workflow of one job, written from the plan the engine computes. It checks the
// repository out, installs Node.js, the packages of each directory the plan names and what their packages ask once
// installed, runs trame ship with the person who started it; shipped, it makes what the graph says it publishes
// available as an artefact of GitHub and records its address as evidence; it keeps the evidence as another artefact,
// whatever the shipment answered, when its settings say so. Nothing is published or kept by default.

/** When a workflow runs, on which machine, with which Node.js: what a project decides for each workflow it ships. */
export interface WorkflowSettings {
  /** What starts it: by hand, a push on these branches, these tags, a schedule (cron, in UTC). */
  readonly on: { readonly manual?: boolean; readonly branches?: readonly string[]; readonly tags?: readonly string[]; readonly schedule?: string }
  /** The machine it runs on, as GitHub names its runners. */
  readonly runsOn: string
  /** The major version of Node.js it installs. */
  readonly node: number
  /**
   * What it does with artefacts of GitHub: how many days GitHub keeps what it publishes (what the workflow's artefact
   * publishes in the graph), and whether it keeps the evidence as another artefact, whatever the shipment answered, for
   * how many days. Without evidence, it keeps none.
   */
  readonly artefacts: { readonly days: number; readonly evidence?: { readonly days: number } }
}

/**
 * GitHub Actions
 * The words of a workflow file: what starts it, what it may do, its jobs, the machine of a job, its steps (an action
 * used and what it is given, a command run and where), the condition of a step; the expression that names the person
 * who started it.
 */


/**
 * The actions a workflow uses
 * The actions of GitHub that check the repository out, install Node.js and keep files, each at the major version a
 * workflow names.
 */


/**
 * A workflow of GitHub Actions
 * The workflow that runs a shipment on GitHub: started as its settings say, its token reading the repository only; one
 * job on the machine they name, whose steps check the repository out, install Node.js, install the packages of each
 * directory of the plan, install the generation's own packages when it has its own, run what the packages ask once
 * installed, and ship with the person who started it. Shipped, what the workflow's artefact publishes in the graph is
 * uploaded as an artefact of GitHub (the deployment, on GitHub), kept as many days as the settings say, and its address
 * recorded as evidence of each artefact, attributed to that person; when the settings keep the evidence, it is uploaded
 * as a second artefact, whatever the shipment answered. It publishes and keeps nothing the graph does not name.
 */

// What the library is for. A project's graph says how a version of it ships (its shipments, their artefacts, checks,
// reviews, deployments), and trame ship runs it here. To run it elsewhere (a service of continuous integration, a
// script on another machine), the engine computes the plan of the run from the graph (what to install, what to prepare,
// the words that ship, what to keep), and a writer of this library gives it the form that place reads. A new place is
// a new writer; the plan, and the graph it comes from, stay the same.

/**
 * A shipment runs elsewhere as it runs here
 * The file a service of continuous integration or another machine runs is written from the graph, by Trame: it
 * prepares what the shipment's programs need, then runs trame ship, which checks, refuses or deploys as it does here.
 * Nobody writes it by hand, so it never says something the graph does not.
 */


/**
 * Each place is a writer
 * A place that runs a shipment (GitHub Actions, a shell, another service tomorrow) is one writer, a syntax element with
 * its examples, written once in this library; projects choose one by naming it as what produces an artefact.
 */

// A shipment run on another machine by a script of Bash: the person who ships gives their name; the script goes to the
// repository's root from where it is, installs the packages of each directory of the plan, installs the generation's own
// packages when it has its own, runs what the packages ask once installed, then ships from the project. It stops at the
// first command that fails.

/**
 * Bash scripts
 * The marks of a script of Bash: the line that names its interpreter, the options that stop it at the first failure (an
 * unset variable, a failing command, a failing pipe), quoting, a subshell, the parameter that refuses to be missing,
 * the directory of the script.
 */


/**
 * A script of Bash
 * The script that runs a shipment on another machine: the person who ships is its first argument, and it stops without
 * one; it goes from its own directory to the repository's root, installs the packages of each directory of the plan,
 * installs the generation's own packages when it has its own, runs what the packages ask once installed, each in its
 * directory, then ships from the project with the person's name.
 */

// The YAML a workflow is written in: a writer builds the document as nodes (mappings with their keys in order,
// sequences, texts, numbers, literal blocks), and one syntax element writes them in the block style.

/**
 * A node of a YAML document, as a writer builds it: a mapping (its keys in order), a sequence, a text, a number, lines
 * kept as they are (a literal block); a node with none of them is empty (a key with nothing after it).
 */
export interface YamlNode {
  readonly map?: readonly (readonly [string, YamlNode])[]
  readonly list?: readonly YamlNode[]
  readonly text?: string
  readonly number?: number
  readonly lines?: readonly string[]
}

/**
 * YAML
 * The marks of a YAML document in the block style: what ends a key, opens an entry of a sequence, opens a literal block,
 * opens a comment, ends a line.
 */


/**
 * A YAML document
 * The comment lines that head it, then its nodes in the block style: a key and its text on one line, a key alone over
 * what it holds, one level deeper; an entry of a sequence after a dash, a mapping in it starting on the dash's line;
 * lines kept as they are in a literal block. A text is written as it is when YAML reads it back the same, otherwise in
 * double quotes, as JSON writes a string (YAML 1.2 reads JSON's strings).
 */
